{
  "case_id": "2005-04-9728",
  "document_hash": "ad3a1122ea0665ceaad8e25f4618b8f184c3b591012b44b09e940a91a30d9f2f",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2005-04-9728",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ad3a1122ea0665ceaad8e25f4618b8f184c3b591012b44b09e940a91a30d9f2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ad3a1122ea06-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:16 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2005-04-9728",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ad3a1122ea0665ceaad8e25f4618b8f184c3b591012b44b09e940a91a30d9f2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ad3a1122ea06-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The search in this case was not based on individualized suspicion, and no other adequate safeguards limited the police officer's discretion to search Petitioner. For this reason, the search, which would not be permitted by virtually any other State or by the Federal Government, was unreasonable."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2005-04-9728",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ad3a1122ea0665ceaad8e25f4618b8f184c3b591012b44b09e940a91a30d9f2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ad3a1122ea06-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But I thought that evidence seized could be introduced, could be a basis to revoke parole."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2005-04-9728",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ad3a1122ea0665ceaad8e25f4618b8f184c3b591012b44b09e940a91a30d9f2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ad3a1122ea06-0000-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the statistics cited in the Government's brief say that in an April 2001 report prepared by the California Criminal Justice Statistics Center, 68 percent of adult parolees are returned to prison -- 68 percent; 55 percent, for a parole violation; and 13 percent for the commission of a new felony offense."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. LONG",
      "case_id": "2005-04-9728",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ad3a1122ea0665ceaad8e25f4618b8f184c3b591012b44b09e940a91a30d9f2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ad3a1122ea06-0000-0004",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "searched at any time, any place, by any police officer for evidence of any crime, is a breathtakingly broad invasion of privacy."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. LONG",
      "case_id": "2005-04-9728",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ad3a1122ea0665ceaad8e25f4618b8f184c3b591012b44b09e940a91a30d9f2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ad3a1122ea06-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I mean, my argument is that what is left of the Fourth Amendment under the California approach is not the core. It's the far periphery. There has never been a case -- and we were able to find over a hundred, and perhaps over 200; it depends on how you count -- of cases where parolees or probationers have said, \"You know, this search was arbitrary or capricious or harassing.\" It is always rejected, for the same reason it was in this case. The Court says, \"Well\" --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2005-04-9728",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ad3a1122ea0665ceaad8e25f4618b8f184c3b591012b44b09e940a91a30d9f2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ad3a1122ea06-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 35,
        "start": 35
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Why is that? Doesn't that kind of beg the question? I mean, if we say he's got the normal Fourth Amendment rights, his -- I mean, the expectation-of-privacy analysis seems to me to be totally circular."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2005-04-9728",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ad3a1122ea0665ceaad8e25f4618b8f184c3b591012b44b09e940a91a30d9f2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ad3a1122ea06-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: Would you say the same thing if the offense he had committed was tax evasion or price-fixing or speeding? Would the -- would a person on parole for any one of those offenses have -- be subject to the same risk of a suspicionless search? And would it be justified?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2005-04-9728",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ad3a1122ea0665ceaad8e25f4618b8f184c3b591012b44b09e940a91a30d9f2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ad3a1122ea06-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: No, just the very fact that there's -- they're an ex-felon."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2005-04-9728",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ad3a1122ea0665ceaad8e25f4618b8f184c3b591012b44b09e940a91a30d9f2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ad3a1122ea06-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 48,
        "start": 48
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: What would be arbitrary and capricious?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2005-04-9728",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ad3a1122ea0665ceaad8e25f4618b8f184c3b591012b44b09e940a91a30d9f2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ad3a1122ea06-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 51,
        "start": 51
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Does it depend -- I mean, what if he's on parole for transporting drugs in balloons from bus stations?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (amicus curiae)",
      "case_id": "2005-04-9728",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ad3a1122ea0665ceaad8e25f4618b8f184c3b591012b44b09e940a91a30d9f2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ad3a1122ea06-0002-0004",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Concerned about the threat that parolees pose to public safety, the California Legislature, in 1996, authorized both police and parole officers to search them without individualized suspicion."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2005-04-9728",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ad3a1122ea0665ceaad8e25f4618b8f184c3b591012b44b09e940a91a30d9f2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ad3a1122ea06-0002-0006",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF JONATHAN L. MARCUS FOR THE UNITED STATES, AS AMICUS CURIAE, IN SUPPORT OF RESPONDENT"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2005-04-9728",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ad3a1122ea0665ceaad8e25f4618b8f184c3b591012b44b09e940a91a30d9f2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ad3a1122ea06-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: What if it's the case --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2005-04-9728",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ad3a1122ea0665ceaad8e25f4618b8f184c3b591012b44b09e940a91a30d9f2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ad3a1122ea06-0002-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: -- your Eighth Amendment rights? You know, if your parole's revoked, you're going to go back into a very cruel and unusual prison, not the one you left."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government (Mr. Marcus)",
      "case_id": "2005-04-9728",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "ad3a1122ea0665ceaad8e25f4618b8f184c3b591012b44b09e940a91a30d9f2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ad3a1122ea06-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 62,
        "start": 62
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. MARCUS: That's correct. And it's to see whether it's objective -- whether the search in this case was objectively related to the purposes of supervision, to protect public safety, and to promote rehabilitation. That, we think, is the test under the Fourth Amendment."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Mr. Long)",
      "case_id": "2005-04-9728",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "ad3a1122ea0665ceaad8e25f4618b8f184c3b591012b44b09e940a91a30d9f2f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ad3a1122ea06-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 65,
        "start": 65
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. LONG: In answer to Justice Alito's question, this search is unconstitutional, because the officer was completely at liberty -- he had complete discretion to decide whether to search."
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
}
